; ============================================================================
;  Paste this ONE line into mIRC's own remote.ini.
;
;  It loads our script at startup and then starts the bridge. Loading is
;  idempotent, so restarting mIRC will not double-load it.
;
;  Do not put the rest of the bridge here: remote.ini is mIRC's file and may
;  already hold your scripts. All our code lives in mirc/bridge.mrc.
; ============================================================================

;  Put bridge.mrc and bridge.ini together, either directly in the mIRC folder or
;  in its scripts\ subfolder. bridge.ini is resolved next to bridge.mrc, so the
;  pair stays portable. If you use scripts\, change the path in the line below.
;  mIRC does not care where a script lives; scripts\ is its convention for the
;  aliases/popups files it ships, not a rule for yours.
on *:START: { if (!$script(bridge.mrc)) { .load -rs $iif($right($mircdir, 1) == $chr(92), $mircdir, $mircdir $+ $chr(92)) $+ bridge.mrc } | bridge.start }

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
;  After pasting:
;
;   1. Copy bridge.mrc and bridge.ini into the folder mIRC actually uses.
;      Ask mIRC:   /echo -a $mircdir
;
;      That is the folder holding YOUR mirc.ini and remote.ini — normally
;      %APPDATA%\mIRC on mIRC 7+. It is NOT the "defaults" folder inside the
;      installation: that one only holds templates mIRC copies on first run,
;      and edits there are ignored.
;
;      (`deno task token` writes bridge.ini; copy it, do not retype the token.)
;   2. Restart mIRC, or run:  /load -rs bridge.mrc  then  /bridge.start
;   3. Watch the daemon log — it records both directions of every frame:
;        deno task log:tail
;
;  If nothing arrives, check in mIRC's status window:
;    /echo -a $sock(bridge)          (empty means the socket is not open)
;    /echo -a $sock(bridge).wserr    (last socket write error)
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
